# nextATT&CKs — environment variables.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE HERE IS OPTIONAL. The product runs with none of them set: no API
# key, no account, no credit card, no network. Copy to `.env` only if you want one
# of the optional behaviours below. Never commit a real key.

# --- Deployment ------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosts (Render, Fly, HF Spaces) inject this. Defaults to 8000 locally.
# PORT=8000

# Stamped into /api/health and into every audit record, so a report can be tied
# back to the build that produced it. Defaults to "dev".
# NEXTATTACK_VERSION=finalist-2026-08-18

# --- Authorisation ---------------------------------------------------------
# UNSET (default): "demo-headers" mode. The caller declares a role in the X-Role
# header. This is authorisation WITHOUT authentication, on purpose — a judge can
# switch roles and watch the API refuse, with zero signup. /api/capabilities
# reports the mode in plain words.
#
# SET: bearer-token mode. Preferred format is
# "token:role:subject:display name" with roles from
# viewer|analyst|responder|admin. Subject is immutable for that credential;
# display name is optional. Tokens are compared in constant time and both the
# declared X-Role and X-Actor headers are ignored. The old token:role format is
# accepted with a pseudonymous token fingerprint as its subject.
# NEXTATTACK_ROLE_TOKENS=change-me-analyst:analyst:asha@soc:Asha,change-me-responder:responder:ravi@soc:Ravi

# Outstanding response proposals are always kept server-side. By default they
# live only for this process, so a restart safely invalidates browser state. Set
# a SQLite path for durable deployments. If NEXTATTACK_AUDIT_DB is configured,
# proposals automatically use a sibling proposals.db unless overridden here.
# NEXTATTACK_PROPOSAL_DB=/var/lib/nextattack/proposals.db

# Durable audit storage. Administrators can rotate a full generation only after
# giving a reason; rotation archives every old row and links the new genesis to
# the sealed head. No API deletes audit history. Without this setting, active and
# archived generations last only for the current process.
# NEXTATTACK_AUDIT_DB=/var/lib/nextattack/audit.db
# NEXTATTACK_PROPOSAL_TTL_SECONDS=1800

# Optional per-entity behavioural history. Until the store contains at least
# seven days, detector scores are labelled diagnostic-only and the server emits
# no operational alerts, severity, attack paths, or response proposals. The UI
# reports learning progress. Once ready, history-backed features become active.
# NEXTATTACK_BASELINE_DB=/var/lib/nextattack/baseline.db
# Readiness requires both distinct active days and events for the organisation,
# account, and source device. These positive-integer policy minimums are optional.
# NEXTATTACK_BASELINE_MIN_ACTIVE_DAYS=7
# NEXTATTACK_BASELINE_MIN_EVENTS_PER_ENTITY=40

# --- Optional language model (OpenAI or Gemini) ----------------------------
# OFF BY DEFAULT, and a key alone will not turn it on. You must set BOTH the
# key and NEXTATTACK_LLM_PROVIDER. That is deliberate: an OPENAI_API_KEY
# exported for some other tool must never start billing you and shipping
# incident text to a third party just because this app happened to run.
#
#   off     (default) no provider, no egress, deterministic templates only
#   auto    use whichever key is present, preferring OpenAI; if that one fails
#           the next provider with a key is tried before giving up
#   openai  require OpenAI  (the default provider, and the one the agent lane
#           is tuned against: it constrains the decoder with a real json_schema)
#   groq    require Groq    (also has decoder-level schema)
#   gemini  require Gemini  (no decoder-level schema here, so it is the last
#           resort for the agent lane rather than its first choice)
#
# A named provider is never silently swapped for another: only `auto` falls
# through. Whatever answers, the deterministic template is always below it, so
# a provider that is down costs prose and never a number.
# NEXTATTACK_LLM_PROVIDER=off

# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-1.5-flash
#
# What enabling this changes: plain-English wording in the Digital Twin advisor
# and incident narrative is written by the model, and the explicit Predict next
# attack button asks it to rank three candidates from a bounded ATT&CK set.
# THIS SENDS INCIDENT-DERIVED TEXT TO THAT PROVIDER, and it costs money at their
# rates. Every score, severity, probability and approval stays deterministic.
# Predicted technique IDs and campaign examples are checked against the bundled
# ATT&CK artifacts, and the forecast is advisory. Both the API payload and UI
# label which provider produced it.
# Check the current state at /api/llm.

# --- Optional threat-intel sources ----------------------------------------
# Both are FREE accounts. Without them the source reports itself as skipped and
# the Threat Radar serves its bundled snapshot. Neither is needed for the demo.
# OTX_API_KEY=
# ABUSECH_AUTH_KEY=
# NVD_API_KEY=

# --- Outbound fetching -----------------------------------------------------
# Extra hosts to add to the SSRF allowlist in src/shared/nethttp.py, comma
# separated. Adding a host here lets the service fetch from it, so treat this as
# a security decision, not a convenience.
# NEXTATTACK_EXTRA_HOSTS=

# --- Frontend --------------------------------------------------------------
# The SPA calls same-origin "/api" in production and relies on the Vite proxy in
# dev. Override only if you split the frontend off the API (we do not recommend
# it — see docs/architecture/adr/0001-networkx-and-single-container.md).
# VITE_API_BASE=/api
# NEXTATTACK_DEV_API=http://127.0.0.1:8001
# VITE_SPLINE_SCENE_URL=https://prod.spline.design/your-scene/scene.splinecode
